steps.message:
## Root cause

`VendorDB.findVendors(String filter)` filtered with a raw, case-sensitive `String.contains()`:

```java
public boolean evaluate(String object) {
    return object.contains(filter);   // case-sensitive
}
```

The vendor database (`res/raw/data.txt`) is stored entirely in upper case — I verified this with `grep -c '[a-z]' data.txt` → **0** matches, for both vendor names and the hex MAC prefixes (which are rendered as `00:16:3E`). So the user's search string had to be upper case to match anything. Typing `00:16:3e` compared against the stored `00:16:3E` and returned nothing, exactly as reported.

## Fix

Normalized the filter and the compared value to upper case at the one place all searching funnels through.

**`VendorUtils.java`** — added a shared, locale-safe upper-casing helper and reused it inside the existing `clean()`:

```java
@NonNull
static String toUpperCase(String source) {
    if (source == null) {
        return StringUtils.EMPTY;
    }
    return source.toUpperCase(Locale.US);
}
```

I used `Locale.US` rather than the no-arg `toUpperCase()` that `clean()` previously called. The no-arg form uses the device default locale, which in Turkish maps `i` → `İ` (dotted capital I) — that would silently break MAC lookups for vendors whose names contain `i`. Hex data is ASCII, so a fixed locale is the correct choice here.

**`VendorDB.java`** — upper-case the filter once in `findVendors`, and make the predicate normalize both sides:

```java
String filterUpperCase = VendorUtils.toUpperCase(filter);
Predicate<String> predicate =
    PredicateUtils.anyPredicate(new StringContains(filterUpperCase), new MacContains(filterUpperCase));
```
```java
private StringContains(@NonNull String filter) {
    this.filter = VendorUtils.toUpperCase(filter);
}

@Override
public boolean evaluate(String object) {
    return VendorUtils.toUpperCase(object).contains(filter);
}
```

Normalizing inside `StringContains` (not just at the `findVendors` entry point) keeps the predicate correct on its own terms, so `MacContains`, which constructs a nested `StringContains`, can't reintroduce the bug. This covers both search paths — MAC addresses *and* vendor names — since the report's "breaks user expectations" complaint applies equally to typing a vendor name in lower case.

## Verification

Added two tests to `VendorDBTest` asserting lower-case and mixed-case queries return results identical to the upper-case query (`00:16:3e` for the MAC path, `cisco systems inc` for the name path).

To confirm the tests actually exercise the defect rather than passing vacuously, I temporarily reverted the two source files and re-ran them:

```
testFindVendorsWithVendorFilterIsCaseInsensitive FAILED
testFindVendorsWithMacFilterIsCaseInsensitive FAILED
```

Both pass with the fix restored. `./gradlew clean assembleDebug testDebugUnitTest` completes with **BUILD SUCCESSFUL** — the full unit test suite passes, including the pre-existing `testFindVendorsWithVendorFilter` / `testFindVendorsWithMacFilter` / `VendorUtilsTest` cases, so upper-case search behavior is unchanged.

One thing I deliberately left alone: searching for an unpunctuated `00163e` still won't match, because the stored display form is colon-separated. That was equally broken before this change and isn't part of the reported bug, so extending the search to strip separators would be a separate behavioral change worth deciding on its own merits.

